but how can i distinguish between a qubit that is not purelyy 0 or 1 amd at the same time not entanglrd
ChatGPT said:

Excellent question 👌 — this is a subtle but very important distinction between superposition and entanglement when looking at Bloch spheres.

1️⃣ Pure |0⟩ or |1⟩

Arrow tip lies exactly on the Bloch sphere surface, along +Z (|0⟩) or −Z (|1⟩).

Length = 1 (max purity).

2️⃣ Pure Superposition (Not Entangled)

Example: 
∣
+
⟩
=
∣
0
⟩
+
∣
1
⟩
2
∣+⟩=
2
	​

∣0⟩+∣1⟩
	​

.

The qubit is still in a pure state, but not |0⟩ or |1⟩.

Arrow still lies on the surface of the Bloch sphere, but tilted away from Z-axis (maybe towards X, Y, or some combination).

Length = 1 again, because it’s still a pure state.

👉 So, on a Bloch sphere, both |0⟩, |1⟩, and superposition states look the same in one sense: the arrow always reaches the surface.
The only difference is the direction of the arrow.

3️⃣ Mixed / Entangled Qubit (Reduced State)

If a qubit is entangled with others, its reduced density matrix is mixed, not pure.

The Bloch vector length will be < 1 (shorter arrow).

That shrinking is the signature of entanglement.

✅ How to Distinguish Superposition vs Entanglement on the Bloch Sphere?

Look at arrow length:

Length = 1 → Pure state (can be |0⟩, |1⟩, or superposition).

Length < 1 → Mixed state (entangled or classically mixed).

Look at arrow direction:

Along +Z → |0⟩.

Along −Z → |1⟩.

Anywhere else on the surface → Superposition (not entangled).

👉 So the rule is:

Position tells you what kind of pure state.

Length tells you if it’s entangled/mixed or not.